WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.837
Good morning, Hank. It's Wednesday.

00:00:01.837 --> 00:00:03.800
When did my house get so nice?!?

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.500
Oh right this isn't my house, this is a hotel room in the great city of Chicago, Illinois

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.720
where I am with David Levithan because we are on tour for Will Grayson, Will Grayson.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.780
Shmancy hotel is shmancy

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.600
So Hank, there's this amazing tumblr that nerdfighters wrote called "effyeah nerdfighters" (Link in the dooblydoo)

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.960
And in the last day a lot of tumblrs - tumblrs? tumbleens? tumbloons?

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.460
Tumblees? Tumbleepoos? Tumbleepoos.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.280
A lot of tumbleepoos have been uploading pictures of themselves holding "Will Grayson, will grayson"

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:37.180
But because of the mirroring of digital images that used to make it so hard for me to figure out how to point to the dooblydoo

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.000
it appears many people are not uploading pictures of themselves with the book called "Will Grayson, will grayson"

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.200
But instead with a book called "Nosyarg Lliw, nosyarg lliw"

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.900
Which, it belatedly occurs to me, would have been a great title.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.620
If we write a sequel it's gonna be called "Nosyarg Lliw, nosyarg lliw"

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:54.260
So last night was the first event of the tour, Hank. It was great - 2 things I have to tell you.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.280
First, there was a stormtrooper.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.300
Not a Nazi stormtrooper, as Star Wars stormtrooper.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.820
There were also a lot of people wearing THIS which is odd because it's hard to focus

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:07.080
on what you're saying when there's a pedophilic pizza loving version of yourself staring back at you

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.780
Oh! Oh AND check this out

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.520
DUN DUN DUNNNNNN

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.840
Nerdfighter: "Yeah, it's pretty awesome."

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.080
John: French the llama!

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.740
Oh and apropos of nothing, here's David and me outside of Frenchy's

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:17.460
John: Here we are!

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:18.440
David: Here we are!

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:19.880
John: At Frenchy's!

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.600
David: Viva the Frenchy's

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.340
John: Only funny if you've read the book

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.960
So speaking of which, your video on Monday (which by the way I loved)

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.300
made me think about the googlization of information in the contemporary world

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.360
I long thought that Google was technically making us stupider as individuals because

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.940
because the internal hard drive inside of our heads is not as needed anymore

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.900
because we have these massive external hard drives that Google can index

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.320
Like, for instance, when I was a kid I know the phone numbers of ALL my friends.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.640
...don't be mean. That's not fair, I had friends!

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.300
Imaginary friends can have imaginary phone numbers okay??

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.480
Jeez, but anyway I knew dozens of imaginary phone numbers, now I know like... four phone numbers

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.960
because I have this amazing external hard drive that stores all the phone numbers I want.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:03.120
And I've been thinking lately, Hank that one of the things long-form narratives, like novels, do is they help us to

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.300
arrange lots of information into a story.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.940
Right, because it's much easier to remember things in a narrative than it is to remember random dislocated facts

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.920
But now that we have these external hard drives that are so good at remembering random facts, we no longer

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:20.020
need to organize information narratively in order to trick our crappy hard drives into remembering it.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.340
And, Hank, I worry that as we spend more time on the Internet

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.320
By the way, Internet, I'm not trying to hurt your feelings here, you know I love you

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.860
Now, don't - don't mad at me, I - I know you're listening

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.600
(whispers): you're always listening

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:35.920
I worry that we will lose interest in, and think we're losing our need for those long-form narratives

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.940
that have so long been a huge part of what it means to be a person.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:43.640
Now, Hank, that worries me partly because my job is writing those long-fom narratives

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.400
but it also worries me for other reasons.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.000
But nerdfighters, I'm interested to know if you find it harder to read books these days.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.500
I know that sometimes I do.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.600
Obviously, I love books, but sometimes even when I'm enjoying a book I find myself putting it down to look at

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.060
the flashy flashy lights of the Internet.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.080
So, nerdfighters, again I hope you like "Will Grayson, will grayson", hope to see a lot of you on tour

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.912
And, Hank, I will see you on Friday.

